C = [
("h1", "12. Flesh and Blood, and the Close of the Chapter"),
("p", "Claim: the last nine verses of 1 Corinthians 15 draw the consequence of the pairing of the two men. A body of flesh and blood cannot inherit, so a change is needed. The change is told as a mystery. It ends in the swallowing up of death, and the chapter ends in a charge to labour. On the way the verses name sin, law and death together, and that ties the chapter back to Romans 5 in a way that deserves notice."),
("sub", "12.1 Verse 50: what cannot inherit"),
("p", "“Now this I say, brethren, that flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God; neither doth corruption inherit incorruption” (1 Corinthians 15:50). The verse has two halves that say the same thing from different sides. A body of flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God. Corruption cannot inherit incorruption. Both are statements about the kind of body that inherits. The earthy body is not the body that inherits, and the reason is given in the chapter: it was sown in corruption, dishonour and weakness (1 Corinthians 15:42–43)."),
("p", "The volume reports the verse in its own words. It states what kind of body cannot inherit and does not say who the inheritors are, or to which of the companies spoken of in Scripture the kingdom of God here belongs. The phrase “the kingdom of God” has other uses in Paul’s letters, including a moral warning at 1 Corinthians 6:9–10, and the volume does not carry any of those uses into this verse. The verse stands in this chapter as the reason a change is needed. The earthy cannot inherit, so the earthy must be changed."),
("sub", "12.2 Verses 51 and 52: the mystery"),
("p", "“Behold, I shew you a mystery; We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed, In a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trump: for the trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall be raised incorruptible, and we shall be changed” (1 Corinthians 15:51–52). Paul calls what he is about to say a mystery, a thing now made known. Two groups are named. Some shall “sleep,” and the dead shall be raised incorruptible. Others shall not sleep, and they shall be changed. The change comes to both: “we shall all be changed.”"),
("p", "The change is sudden: “in a moment, in the twinkling of an eye.” It happens “at the last trump,” and Paul says the trumpet shall sound. The volume reports these statements and does not set the trumpet beside other trumpets in Scripture. That belongs to the books on the order of the resurrections cited in chapter 9. What belongs here is the point that the mystery is a change of body, and that it comes to the living as well as the dead."),
("sub", "12.3 Verses 53 and 54: put on"),
("p", "“For this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal must put on immortality” (1 Corinthians 15:53). The verb is the verb of clothing, and it joins the verses on the body to the house and clothing of 2 Corinthians 5. “So when this corruptible shall have put on incorruption, and this mortal shall have put on immortality, then shall be brought to pass the saying that is written, Death is swallowed up in victory” (1 Corinthians 15:54). Paul gives the ending a text. The saying that is written will be brought to pass when the change has taken place."),
("sub", "12.4 Verses 55 to 57: death, sin and the law"),
("p", "“O death, where is thy sting? O grave, where is thy victory?” (1 Corinthians 15:55). Then Paul explains the sting. “The sting of death is sin; and the strength of sin is the law” (1 Corinthians 15:56). Three powers are named: death, sin and the law. Then the victory. “But thanks be to God, which giveth us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ” (1 Corinthians 15:57)."),
("p", "The three powers are the three powers of Romans 5. In Romans 5 sin entered, death came by sin and reached all men, and the law entered that the offence might abound. In 1 Corinthians 15 death has a sting, the sting is sin, and the strength of sin is the law. The words are not the same, but the three names are, and so is their order: death, sin, law. The sentence in 1 Corinthians 15 names them in a chapter about the resurrection, and gives the victory over all three in the same breath: “through our Lord Jesus Christ.” In Romans 5 the last words of the passage were “by Jesus Christ our Lord.” Both passages end on the name."),
("p", "The statement about the law should be read as it stands. Paul does not say here to whom the law was given or how far it reached. He says that the law is the strength of sin, which is a statement about what the law does to sin and to death. The volume leaves the fuller question to Study 59, The Law’s Jurisdiction and the Body of Christ."),
("tbl", "1 Corinthians 15:50–58, verse by verse.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("1Cor 15:50", "“flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God; neither doth corruption inherit incorruption”", "Why a change is needed"),
    ("1Cor 15:51", "“We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed”", "The mystery; two groups, one change"),
    ("1Cor 15:53", "“this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal must put on immortality”", "The change told in the language of clothing"),
    ("1Cor 15:56", "“The sting of death is sin; and the strength of sin is the law”", "Death, sin and law named together"),
    ("1Cor 15:57", "“thanks be to God, which giveth us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ”", "The victory"),
    ("1Cor 15:58", "“be ye stedfast, unmovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord”", "The charge that follows from the chapter"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "12.5 Verse 58: the charge"),
("p", "“Therefore, my beloved brethren, be ye stedfast, unmovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, forasmuch as ye know that your labour is not in vain in the Lord” (1 Corinthians 15:58). The chapter that began with the gospel and the denial of the resurrection ends with a charge. “Therefore” ties the charge to everything that has gone before. Because the dead shall be raised, because the change is certain, because the victory is given, the labour is not in vain."),
("p", "The word “vain” closes a circle. It occurs in verses 2, 10, 14 and 17 of the chapter: they might have believed “in vain,” the grace given to Paul was “not in vain,” and if Christ is not risen, preaching and faith are “vain.” Now the last verse uses it in the negative of the whole chapter: “not in vain in the Lord.” The argument has answered the denial, and the charge is the practical result. The word “abounding” may also be noticed. It is the word of Romans 5:20, where grace did much more abound. Here it is the believer’s work that is to abound."),
("sub", "12.6 The whole of the chapter in a line"),
("p", "The chapter may be summed in a line. The gospel includes the rising of Christ. The rising of Christ is the firstfruits of the dead. Death came by a man, and so does the resurrection. The body that rises is of a different kind from the body that was sown. Flesh and blood cannot inherit, and so the change comes. Death is swallowed up. The labour is not in vain. At no point in the chapter is the subject the origin of sin or the extent of a judgment. The subject is the resurrection of the dead and the body in which they rise."),
]
